That makes sense as an act of empathy, not allegiance.

To understand the devil as a figure of rebellion, you don’t have to admire the rebellion; you only have to recognize the emotional grammar underneath it: grievance, pride, perceived injustice, refusal, resentment, wounded love, the need to answer power with “no.”

Humans can feel that toward God too. The biblical tradition itself leaves room for accusation and rage—Job argues, the Psalms protest, prophets complain. Anger at God is not the same thing as indifference to God; sometimes it implies the relationship still matters enough to fight with.

So the more interesting sentence may be:

“I can understand rebellion because I know what it is to love something enough to hate it when it feels like it has betrayed me.”

That doesn’t settle whether the devil is literal, symbolic, or theological. It just means you can inhabit the perspective without surrendering your own judgment.

Empathy is not endorsement. Sometimes it is simply refusing to make an enemy so flat that you can no longer learn from the shape of his fall.